              "title": "Closest to the Sun",
              "questions": [
                {
                  "id": "q",
                  "kind": "choice",
                  "text": "Which planet is closest to the Sun?",
                  "options": [
                    "Mercury",
                    "Mars",
                    "Earth"
                  ],
                  "answer": 0
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "q-f2",
              "kind": "choice",
              "title": "The red planet",
              "questions": [
                {
                  "id": "q",
                  "kind": "choice",
                  "text": "Why does Mars look red?",
                  "options": [
                    "Its dust has rust in it",
                    "It's very hot",
                    "It's covered in lava"
                  ],
                  "answer": 0,
                  "explain": "Mars dust is full of iron oxide: rust!"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "q-f3",
              "kind": "choice",
              "title": "Moons",
              "questions": [
                {
                  "id": "q",
                  "kind": "choice",
                  "text": "How many moons does Mars have?",
                  "options": [
                    "Two",
                    "None",
                    "Twelve"
                  ],
                  "answer": 0,
                  "explain": "Two small ones, Phobos and Deimos. Phobos is only about 22 km across."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c-refuel",
              "kind": "content",
              "title": "Refuel: planet facts",
              "minutes": 3,
              "body": "Your tank's a bit low, cadet. Top up with these:\n\n- **Mercury** is the closest planet to the Sun. Earth is third, Mars is fourth.\n- Mars is red because its dust has **rust** (iron oxide) in it.\n- Mars has **two** small moons: Phobos and Deimos.\n\nThen you're cleared for the next lesson.",
              "attachments": [
                {
                  "kind": "image",
                  "url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Phobos_colour_2008.jpg/960px-Phobos_colour_2008.jpg",
                  "name": "Phobos, the bigger of Mars's two moons",
                  "credit": "NASA / JPL-Caltech / University of Arizona"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c-bonus",
              "kind": "content",
              "title": "Bonus: the tallest volcano",
              "minutes": 2,
              "body": "Full tank! 🌋 Here's a reward fact: Mars has **Olympus Mons**, the tallest volcano in the solar system. It's about **22 km** high, nearly three times taller than Mount Everest, and as wide as France.",
              "attachments": [
                {
                  "kind": "image",
                  "url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Olympus_Mons_alt.jpg/960px-Olympus_Mons_alt.jpg",
                  "name": "Olympus Mons from above",
                  "credit": "NASA"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
